{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "awsbridge",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-21",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  "observability": { "enabled": true },
  // The profile list (the accounts selectable with `aws_profile`) is bundled as a JSON module, because a Worker environment variable is limited to 5 KB.
  // src/index.ts imports "awsbridge:profiles"; this alias points it at config/profiles.json, which is gitignored.
  // Create it first: `cp config/profiles.example.json config/profiles.json` and edit it (see README.md "Profiles").
  // Without the file, `wrangler deploy` / `wrangler dev` fail to resolve the module (that is the "not configured" error).
  // Downstream repositories do not edit this file: their own `wrangler.<name>.jsonc` sets `alias` to their own JSON file.
  "alias": { "awsbridge:profiles": "./config/profiles.json" },
  // ISSUER (public URL of the Worker) is passed at deploy time with `--var ISSUER:<url>`; it is not stored in this repo.
  // Secrets (SIGNING_JWK, ADDITIONAL_PUBLIC_JWKS, ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN, ACCESS_AUD) are registered with `mise run secrets` (wrangler secret bulk).
  // The profile list is NOT an env var / secret any more (AWS_PROFILES was removed): see "alias" above and config/profiles.example.json.
  // Format: { "default": "dev-readonly", "profiles": [ { "name": ..., "roleArn": ..., "description"?: ... } ], "tools"?: [...] }
  // `profiles` is an array (recommended): its order becomes the order of `list_aws_profiles` (the default profile always comes first).
  // Environments (select one with AWSBRIDGE_WRANGLER_ENV; the default is `dev`).
  //   prod: the basic form. Custom Domains on your own zone (replace example.com). The issuer host and the connection host are separate:
  //     awsbridge-oidc-mcp.example.com  the OIDC issuer (the URL of the IAM OIDC provider). Set AWSBRIDGE_ISSUER=https://awsbridge-oidc-mcp.example.com (no path).
  //                           Open /.well-known/* to the public (Access bypass) because STS reads it. Any other path (/mcp) is 401 here (the Worker checks the Access JWT).
  //     awsbridge-mcp.example.com      where MCP clients connect (/mcp). The Access application protects this host.
  //     One Worker serves both hosts. workers.dev and Preview URLs are closed (entrances that skip Access).
  //     The issuer host is what the IAM OIDC provider trusts: a domain that expires, is unlocked at the registrar, or sits in a zone others can edit lets a third party issue tokens. Protect it like a signing key.
  //   dev: a quick trial on workers.dev (no domain needed). The issuer is https://<worker>.<subdomain>.workers.dev. Use prod for real use.
  "env": {
    "prod": {
      "name": "awsbridge",
      "workers_dev": false,
      "preview_urls": false,
      "routes": [
        { "pattern": "awsbridge-mcp.example.com", "custom_domain": true },
        { "pattern": "awsbridge-oidc-mcp.example.com", "custom_domain": true }
      ]
    },
    "dev": {
      "name": "awsbridge-dev",
      "workers_dev": true
    }
  }
}
